Hi Taku.

On Wed, Apr 7, 2010 at 7:00 PM, tokuyasu <sock...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Dear All,
>
> On a different note, could you please confirm that the various
> versions of the CDF files have no impact on the actual mapping of data
> to annotations?

A Chip Definition File (CDF) contains annotation information that
maps/groups probes on the chip to different genomic entities of
interest.  See also

http://aroma-project.org/definitions/chipTypesAndCDFs

There may be different CDF for the same chip type, e.g. you can design
one gene-centric CDF that is custom made for gene expression analysis
and one exon-centric CDF that is made for alternative-splicing
analysis.   See for instance

  http://aroma-project.org/chipTypes/HuEx-1_0-st-v2

> I am working on a MoGene 1.0 dataset where a
> diferentially expressed gene (confirmed by PCR) does not show up as
> such on the array, so I would like to be 100% certain there is no
> possibility of misalignment.

It is not possible to comment more on your question without knowing
which CDF you are using, and what other (MoGene-1_0-st-v1?) CDFs you
are also considering.

Cheers,

Henrik

